2] \u2014 Red Dragon pung.\n\nAcross has [B3,B4,B5], [W7,W8,W9]\n\nNext player has no melds.\n\nI discarded J2 earlier \u2014 safe.\n\nNow, **evaluation of moves**:\n\n- **Play T9**: removes an isolated terminal tile. Doesn't damage any structure. Opens possibility to draw towards pair or improve chows.\n- **Play F2**: loses chance for seat wind pair. But F2 is honor, less likely to help in chow.\n- **Play B6/B7/B8/W6/W7/W8**: all break potential [B6,B7,B8] or [W6,W7,W8] chows. Since I already have [B5,B6,B7] and [W4,W5,W6], keeping B6,B7,B8 and W6,W7,W8 allows flexibility.\n\nBut I can't keep all.\n\nActually, holding both [W6,W7,W8] and [B6,B7,B8] uses 6 tiles, leaving T9 and F2 \u2014 no pair.\n\nSo I must discard one of the group, or one of the singles.\n\nBut if I discard, say, B8, then I have [B6,B7] \u2014 waiting for B5 or B8 to complete chow.\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nBut better to keep the stronger structure.\n\nNote: I already have [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 so W6 is used.\n\nThe W6 in hand is extra \u2014 so I can use it for [W6,W7,W8].\n\nSimilarly, B6 in hand can be used for [B6,B7,B8].\n\nSo I can have:\n- Meld 1: [W4,W5,W6] (exposed)\n- Meld 2: [B5,B6,B7] (exposed)\n- Meld 3: [W6,W7,W8] (concealed) \u2014 requires W6,W7,W8\n- Meld 4: [B6,B7,B8] (concealed) \u2014 requires B6,B7,B8\n- Wait: but W6, B6, B7 are used in two places? No \u2014 I have only one copy each in hand.\n\nHand: W6, W7, W8, B6, B7, B8, T9, F2 \u2014 so one of each.\n\nSo I cannot form both [W6,W7,W8] and [B6,B7,B8] because that would require two W6s, etc. \u2014 I don't.\n\nI have only one W6, one B6, etc.\n\nSo I must choose which chow to complete.\n\nOptions:\n- Complete [W6,W7,W8] \u2192 then B6,B7,B8 become scattered\n- Or complete [B6,B7,B8] \u2192 W6,W7,W8 scattered\n\nBut I already have [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 so B6 and B7 are already used in a meld.\n\nThe B6 and B7 in hand are additional copies \u2014 so I can use them.\n\nYes \u2014 tile count allows.\n\nSo I can have:\n- [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 uses one W6\n- [W6,W7,W8] \u2014 uses another W6, plus W7,W8\n- [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 uses one B6, one B7\n- [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 uses another B6, B7, and B8\n\nIs that legal? Only if I have two W6, two B6, two B7.\n\nCheck:\n\nW6: total 4\n- My meld: 1\n- My hand: 1 \u2192 total 2\n- Previous player discarded W6: 1\n- Unshown: 2 \u2192 so yes, two copies in play, I can have one in meld, one in hand\n\nB6:\n- My meld: 1\n- My hand: 1\n- Across player discarded B6: 1\n- Unshown: 1 \u2192 total 4 \u2192 ok\n\nB7:\n- My meld: 1\n- My hand: 1\n- Across player discarded B7: 1\n- Unshown: 2 \u2192 ok\n\nSo yes \u2014 I have enough copies.\n\nTherefore, I can legally form:\n- [W4,W5,W6] (exposed)\n- [B5,B6,B7] (exposed)\n- [W6,W7,W8] (concealed chow)\n- [B6,B7,B8] (concealed chow)\n\nTiles used:\n- W: W4,W5,W6 (meld), W6,W7,W8 (chow) \u2192 W4,W5,W6,W6,W7,W8\n- B: B5,B6,B7 (meld), B6,B7,B8 (chow) \u2192 B5,B6,B7,B6,B7,B8\n- Then left with T9 and F2\n\nStill no pair.\n\nAnd I have 14 tiles: 4 melds \u00d7 3 = 12, plus 2 for pair \u2014 but here I have 12 in melds, and 2 singles \u2014 not a pair.\n\nSo I need to discard one of T9 or F2 to go to 13 tiles, then wait to win by drawing the other.\n\nBut currently I have 8 live tiles + 2 melds \u00d7 3 = 6 \u2192 14 tiles? No.\n\nStandard: hand has 13 tiles before draw.\n\nAfter draw, 14, then discard.\n\nCurrently, I have 8 live tiles, and 2 melds (6 tiles) \u2192 total 14 \u2014 so I just drew.\n\nI must discard one.\n\nAfter discard, I will have 13 tiles: 12 in melds? No \u2014 melds are separate.\n\nNo: melds are part of hand.\n\nTotal tiles in hand: 13.\n\nHere: 2 melds = 6 tiles, live hand = 8 \u2192 14 tiles \u2014 impossible.\n\nAh, mistake.\n\nIn Mahjong, after drawing, you have 14 tiles, then discard to 13.\n\nSo current state: I have just drawn a tile, now have 14 tiles: 6 in two melds, 8 in hand.\n\nI must discard one of the 8.\n\nValid actions are the discards.\n\nSo after discard, I will have 13 tiles: 6 in melds, 7 in hand.\n\nThen next turn, draw to 14, discard, etc.\n\nSo I cannot have four melds yet.\n\nI must keep working on two melds.\n\nMy goal is to form two more melds and a pair.\n\nCurrent hand: W6, W7, W8, T9, B6, B7, B8, F2\n\nI have two potential chows: [W6,W7,W8] and [B6,B7,B8]\n\nBut I can only complete one, since they use different tiles.\n\nIf I keep both, I can eventually complete both, but I need to discard something else.\n\nBest discard: **T9** or **F2**\n\nT9 is a terminal, less flexible.\n\nF2 is seat wind \u2014 potentially valuable for pattern.\n\nBut I have no other F2.\n\nAlso, prevalent wind is East (F1), not my seat wind.\n\nSo Seat Wind pattern requires a pung or kong of F2 \u2014 I only have one.\n\nSo 2 points if I get a pung.\n\nBut unlikely.\n\nMore practical: reduce dead weight.\n\nT9 is completely isolated.\n\nNo chow involves T9 except [T7,T8,T9] or [T8,T9,T9], etc.\n\nT7, T8: previous player discarded T8, T1, T2, etc.\n\nUnshown T9: 3 \u2014 so possible.\n\nBut I have no T7 or T8.\n\nSo T9 is useless.\n\nSimilarly, F2: only way is to pair it.\n\nBut I can draw another.\n\nBut low probability.\n\nWhereas W6,W7,W8 can form a chow, similarly B6,B7,B8.\n\nBut I already have [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 so if I keep W6,W7,W8, I have two W chows: 456 and 678 \u2014 good for Pure Straight if I had 123 or 789, but I don't.\n\nSimilarly for B: 567 and 678.\n\nOverlap.\n\nBut [W6,W7,W8] is a strong keep.\n\nSimilarly [B6,B7,B8].\n\nBut I can keep both \u2014 they don't conflict.\n\nW6,W7,W8 and B6,B7,B8 use distinct tiles.\n\nYes.\n\nSo I can aim to have:\n- [W4,W5,W6] (exposed)\n- [B5,B6,B7] (exposed)\n- [W6,W7,W8] (to be completed)\n- [B6,B7,B8] (to be completed)\n\nThen I need a pair from T9 and F2.\n\nSo I must discard one of T9 or F2 now.\n\nWhich one?\n\nT9 is terminal, F2 is honor.\n\nBoth can be part of Outside Hand, but I have many simples.\n\nBetter to keep F2 \u2014 honors are rarer, and F2 could pair.\n\nT9 is more common, but also riskier.\n\nSince I have no use for either, and both are equally bad, but **F2 is my seat wind**, so slightly more valuable if paired.\n\nBut for now, discarding either doesn't lose much.\n\nBut looking at opponents:\n\n- Across player has [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 so they have W7,W8,W9.\n\nThey may be waiting for W6 or W10 (invalid) \u2014 so W6 might be dangerous.\n\nSimilarly, they might want W6 for [W6,W7,W8].\n\nSo if I discard W6, it might feed them.\n\nSimilarly, B6,B7,B8: across has [B3,B4,B5], no high B.\n\nPrevious player has dragons and T2.\n\nNext player has no melds.\n\nSo B6,B7,B8 safer.\n\nBut I'm not discarding them.\n\nI'm considering discarding T9 or F2.\n\nT9: who wants it?\n\nPrevious player has [T2,T2,T2] \u2014 maybe going for Terminals.\n\nBut T2 only.\n\nAcross has no T.\n\nNext player: history shows T3,T4,T6,T7,T8 \u2014 so may have T.\n\nUnshown T9: 3 \u2014 so not many out.\n\nBut possible someone waits for [T7,T8,T9].\n\nBut less likely.\n\nF2: South Wind.\n\nPrevalent is East.\n\nNo one has shown F2.\n\nPrevious player has F1, F3, F4 \u2014 so may be collecting winds.\n\nDiscarding F2 might be safe.\n\nBut better to keep it for potential pair.\n\nSo best to discard **T9** \u2014 it's the most useless tile.\n\nIt doesn't contribute to any pattern, while F2 has slight value.\n\nMoreover, discarding T9 keeps open the possibility of pairing F2 later.\n\nAlso, if I draw a W or B tile, I can improve.\n\nAnswer: Play T9\n\nFinal decision.\n\nSituation: I have two exposed chows and a hand rich in middle-numbered suited tiles, particularly W6-W8 and B6-B8. I hold duplicates of several tiles, allowing potential for two additional chows. However, I lack a pair, with only T9 and F2 as singleton candidates. T9 is a terminal with no connections, while F2 is my seat wind, offering slight pattern value.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding T9 removes a dead tile without disrupting my strong chow-building potential in both Character and Bamboo suits. Keeping F2 preserves a chance for the Seat Wind pattern (2 points) if I draw another. Discarding any W or B tile risks feeding opponents or weakening my own structure.\n\nReason: T9 is the least valuable tile in hand, contributing to no meld and having the lowest potential for future utility. Removing it streamlines my hand toward a viable All Chows or Full Flush-like hand with higher scoring potential.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
