- [W6, W7, W8]: formed via Chi (history: \"Chi W7\")\n\nBut to form [W6, W7, W8], I must have had W6 and W8 in hand and claimed W7 discarded by the prior player. However, W6 was already used in [W4, W5, W6] \u2014 unless that meld is actually [W4, W5, W6] using a different copy.\n\nGiven W6 has 4 copies: let's track usage.\n\nFrom game histories:\n- I played W3, W5, W7, W9, W2 \u2014 but not W6 directly.\n- Previous player played W6 once.\n- Across player played W6 once.\n- Next player played W7 twice \u2014 possibly indicating W6 nearby.\n\nUnshown W6: 1 \u2192 so three are already out.\n\nMy melds use W6 in two places \u2192 requires two W6s in my melds \u2192 possible if I have two.\n\nBut my current hand does **not** contain W6 \u2192 so both W6s must be in melds.\n\nTotal W6: 4 copies.\n- Used in my [W4,W5,W6] \u2192 1\n- Used in my [W6,W7,W8] \u2192 1 \u2192 total 2 used by me\n- Unshown: 1 \u2192 so one still available\n- One other played/discarded \u2192 consistent\n\nSo it's feasible.\n\nNow, my **current hand**: [W2, W8, B5, B6, B7]\n\nWait \u2014 B5, B6, B7 are in a meld [B5,B6,B7], so they shouldn't be in hand. Contradiction.\n\nAh! This is critical: **melds are separate from hand**. So if [B5,B6,B7] is a meld, then those tiles are no longer in hand.\n\nBut the system says: \"Your hand tiles are: [W2: 1; W8: 1; B5: 1; B6: 1; B7: 1]\" \u2014 which includes B5, B6, B7 \u2014 but also lists [B5,B6,B7] as a meld.\n\nThis is impossible unless four copies exist and we're double-using.\n\nCheck B5: total 4 copies.\n- Unshown: B5:1 \u2192 so 3 already out\n- I claim to have B5 in hand AND in meld \u2192 at least 2 copies used by me\n- Across player played B5 twice? From history: across played B5 once\n- Previous player played B5 once\n- I played B5? No \u2014 I played B4 twice, B8, etc.\n\nPossibly:\n- My meld [B5,B6,B7] uses one B5\n- My hand has another B5 \u2192 second copy\n- Across played one B5\n- Previous played one B5 \u2192 total 4 \u2192 okay\n\nSame f